chapters.referenceText:
What You Will Learn in This Chapter
Hashing is one of the most important coding interview patterns after basic array
traversal.
Basic traversal lets you remember a few variables:
sum
count
maxVal
minVal
best
But many interview problems require remembering many values and checking them
quickly.
Hashing helps you answer questions like:

Have I seen this value before?

How many times has this value appeared?

What index did this value appear at?

Does the complement exist?

Are two strings made of the same character frequencies?

Can I group items by a common signature?

How many previous prefix sums match what I need?

Which elements are common between two arrays?
By the end of this chapter, you should be comfortable with:

unordered_map

unordered_set

frequency counting

duplicate detection

complement lookup

value-to-index mapping

grouping by key

character frequency arrays

prefix sum + hash map

hash set expansion

common edge cases

common C++ hash map mistakes
