6. Full Problem Walkthrough 1
LeetCode 155: Min Stack

6.1 Problem Summary
Design stack supporting:
push
pop
top
getMin
All in O(1).
6.2 Key Idea
Maintain another stack of current minimum values.
At every push, store:
value
current minimum so far
6.3 C++ Code
class MinStack {
private:
stack<pair<int, int>> st;
public:
MinStack() {
}
void push(int val) {
int currentMin = val;
if (!st.empty()) {
currentMin = min(currentMin, st.top().second);
}
st.push({val, currentMin});
}
void pop() {
st.pop();
}
int top() {
return st.top().first;
}
int getMin() {
return st.top().second;

}
};
6.4 Why This Works
Each stack entry remembers the minimum at the moment it was pushed.
When popped, previous minimum is automatically restored.
7. Full Problem Walkthrough 2
Max Stack
7.1 Problem Summary
Design stack supporting:
push
pop
top
peekMax
popMax
popMax removes the top-most maximum if duplicates exist.
7.2 Approach
Use:
list<int> dll
map<int, vector<list<int>::iterator>> positions
Why?

list supports O(1) erase by iterator.

map gives largest value using rbegin.

vector stores iterators for each value in stack order.
7.3 C++ Code
class MaxStack {
private:
list<int> dll;
map<int, vector<list<int>::iterator>> pos;
public:
MaxStack() {
}
void push(int x) {
dll.push_back(x);
auto it = prev(dll.end());
pos[x].push_back(it);
}

int pop() {
int x = dll.back();
dll.pop_back();
pos[x].pop_back();
if (pos[x].empty()) {
pos.erase(x);
}
return x;
}
int top() {
return dll.back();
}
int peekMax() {
return pos.rbegin()->first;
}
int popMax() {
auto mapIt = prev(pos.end());
int maxVal = mapIt->first;
auto listIt = mapIt->second.back();
dll.erase(listIt);
mapIt->second.pop_back();
if (mapIt->second.empty()) {
pos.erase(mapIt);
}
return maxVal;
}
};
7.4 Complexity

push:
O(log n)
pop:
O(log n)
top:
O(1)
peekMax: O(1)
popMax: O(log n)
